  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 115 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nWednesday night through Thursday night\nAssessment...medium to high (60-80%) confidence of a storm system.\nLow (10-20%) confidence on overall impacts from precipitation.\n\nThe degree of phasing is the key as to what impacts will be seen.\n\nThe models continue the trend toward more phasing of the system but \ndiffer on the amount. The system is negatively tilted thus non-\nlinear processes will dominate resulting in the models playing catch \nup with each run cycle. Since the system is negatively tilted, if \nthe amount of phasing continues to increase then the probability of \na westward shift with the precipitation would increase.\n\nWhat has changed from 24 hours ago is that the models are showing a \nbit more dynamic cooling. Thus the prospects of seeing light snow \nhas increased. HOWEVER, as mentioned yesterday the exceedingly warm \nground and pavement temperatures will need to be overcome by high \nsnowfall rates in order to get any accumulation.\n\nThe models agree that an inverted trof will be over the area helping \nto create the lift and convergence to generate precipitation. At the \nsame time (albeit not as strong as yesterday) the models are trying \nto warm temperatures aloft which would suggest an eventual rain/snow \nmix developing, especially during the day Thursday.\n\nThere appears to be a 6-9 hour time frame, roughly midnight to 9 AM \nThursday, where the precipitation type would be all snow. If the \ntrend of warming temperatures aloft continues, then by late morning \na change over to a mix if not all rain would most likely occur. IF \nthe dynamic cooling continues or strengthens, then light snow would \nlikely continue through at least mid-day Thursday.\n\nWhere the precipitation lays out depends upon the amount of phasing. \nHOWEVER, areas generally along/east of the Mississippi are still \nfavored which is where the model consensus has the highest \nprecipitation chances.\n\nAs mentioned earlier, snowfall rates would need to be an inch or \nhigher per hour in order to overcome the very warm ground and \npavement temperatures to generate any accumulation. While high \nresolution hourly rates are not yet available, the probability of \nseeing an inch or more of snow over a 6 hour period is 20-40 percent \ngenerally east of a Mount Carroll, IL to Kewanee, IL line. West of \nthat line and to the Mississippi, the probabilities of an inch or \nmore of snow are 5-15 percent.\n\nThe models are suggesting a 4-5 hour window, roughly 3 and 8 AM \nThursday, where snowfall rates may be at their highest. IF correct \nthen a dusting of accumulation may be possible along/east of the \nMississippi prior to sunrise Thursday.\n\nThursday afternoon/evening the precipitation will most likely be in \nthe form of light rain and will slowly end from west to east as the \nsystem moves into the eastern Great Lakes.\n \nCompared to yesterday, the overall potential for minor impacts have \nhas increased to 20-30 percent for areas along/east of the \nMississippi.",
